--- !u!1001 &2956303667684457623
PrefabInstance:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  serializedVersion: 2
  m_Modification:
    serializedVersion: 3
    m_TransformParent: {fileID: 0}
    m_Modifications:
    - target: {fileID: 159493454401179885, guid: 6a3b6aa2e571ae041b78fd60af199e5e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_Radius
      value: 1.5
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 3357063327598446276, guid: 6a3b6aa2e571ae041b78fd60af199e5e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_Text
      value: The Burmese Python (Python bivittatus) is an invasive species of predator
        which has quickly taken over the Everglades. Because of their large size,
        adult Burmese pythons have few predators, with humans being the exception.
        They prey upon native species and may reduce their populations locally. Severe
        declines in mammal populations throughout the Everglades have been linked
        to Burmese pythons, with the most severe declines in native species having
        occurred in the remote southernmost regions of the Everglades where pythons
        have been established the longest. As one of the largest snakes in the world,
        they can grow to impressive sizes. Adult Burmese pythons caught in Florida
        average between 6 and 9 feet. Generally their diet consists of various mammals,
        birds and reptiles.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
